"[2201.10030] The Pop-stack-sorting Operator on Tamari Lattices (https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.10030)\nciteturn9512view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.10030\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL9/Mansour/mansour86.pdf)\nciteturn9512view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 13; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL9/Mansour/mansour86.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1295\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPop-Stack Operators for Torsion Classes and Cambrian Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1)\nciteturn9512view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 5.3.\"}); Total lines: 1931\nL113: Our first main result (cite86†Theorem 5.1 ) states that, under the bijection between $\\tors\\Lambda$ and $\\smc\\Lambda$, applying the pop-stack operator and its dual to a torsion class corresponds to performing certain mutations on the associated 2-term simple-minded collections.\nL120: It turns out that our representation-theoretic perspective is quite useful for understanding the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}$. In cite91†Theorem 5.10 , we provide a representation-theoretic characterization of the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ whenever $\\Lambda$ is hereditary.\nL547: In cite21†Section 5.2 , we describe preimages under the pop-stack operators. In cite22†Section 5.3 , we describe the images of the pop-stack operators.\nL548: ### 5.1. Pop-stack and mutation\nL549: \nL550: We now prove our first main result, which describes the relationship between the pop-stack operators and the mutation of 2-term simple-minded collections.\nL551: \nL552: ###### Theorem 5.1.\nL553: \nL554: For $(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})\\in\\smc\\Lambda$, let $\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y}):=\\mu_{\\mathcal{X}}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})$ and $\\mu_{\\uparrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y}):=\\mu_{\\mathcal{Y}}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})$. There are commutative diagrams as follows:\nL555: \nL556:  |  |\nL557: ###### Proof.\nL558: \nL559: Let $(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})\\in\\smc\\Lambda$. Then by cite96†Theorem 4.6 cite125†5 and cite124†Corollary 3.6 , we have that\nL560:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathsf{T}(\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y}))$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X})\\cap\\prescript{\\perp}{}{\\mathcal{X}}$  |\nL561:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X})\\cap\\left(\\bigcap\\left\\{\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X})\\cap\\prescript{\\perp}{}{X}\\mid X\\in\\mathcal{X}\\right\\}\\right)$  |\nL562:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}(\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})).$  |\nL563: This shows that the left diagram commutes. Similarly, cite96†Theorem 4.6 cite126†5 and cite124†Corollary 3.6 imply that\nL564:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathsf{T}(\\mu_{\\uparrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y}))$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\Filt(\\prescript{\\perp}{}{\\mathcal{Y}}\\cup\\mathcal{Y})$  |\nL565:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\Filt\\left(\\prescript{\\perp}{}{\\mathcal{Y}}\\cup\\left(\\bigcup\\left\\{\\Filt(\\prescript{\\perp}{}{\\mathcal{Y}}\\cup Y)\\mid Y\\in\\mathcal{Y}\\right\\}\\right)\\right)$  |\nL566:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}(\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})).$  |\nL567: This shows that the right diagram commutes. ∎\nL568: ###### Remark 5.2.\nL569: One can also generalize the proof of cite86†Theorem 5.1 to show that the torsion classes of the form $\\mathsf{T}(\\mu_{\\mathcal{X}^{\\prime}}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y}))$ (with $\\mathcal{X}^{\\prime}\\subseteq\\mathcal{X}$) are precisely those obtained by intersecting $\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X})$ with a subset of the torsion classes it covers.\nL570: (The dual result likewise holds for right mutation.) In the language of [cite127†DL , cite128†DKW ], these are precisely the torsion classes obtained by applying Ungar moves to $\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X})$. Intervals of the form $[\\mathsf{T}(\\mu_{\\mathcal{X}^{\\prime}}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})),\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X})]$ have also appeared in many of the previously-cited papers on lattices of torsion classes such as [cite46†AP22 , cite50†Hana ].\nL571: ### 5.2. Preimages under the pop-stack operators\nL572: \nL573: We now characterize the preimages of a given torsion class under the pop-stack operator and its dual. As a consequence, we describe the 1-pop-stack sortable elements and the 2-pop-stack sortable elements.\nL574: ###### Theorem 5.3.\nL575: \nL576: Let $\\mathcal{T},\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$. Then $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$ if and only if both of the following hold.\nL577: \nL578:   1. (1)\nL579: \nL580: There is an inclusion $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL581: \nL582:   2. (2)\nL583: \nL584: For every $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$ admitting a surjection $Z\\twoheadrightarrow X$.\nL585: Moreover, if $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$, then $(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))$.\nL586: \nL587: Dually, $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}$ if and only if both of the following hold.\nL588: \nL589:   1. (1)\nL590: \nL591: There is an inclusion $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})$.\nL592: \nL593:   2. (2)\nL594: For every $Y\\in\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))$ admitting an injection $X\\hookrightarrow Z$.\nL595: \nL596: Moreover, if $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}$, then $(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=\\mu_{\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$.\nL597: ###### Proof.\nL598: \nL599: We prove only the first half of the theorem; the proof of the second half is dual.\nL600: \nL601: First suppose $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$. Then\nL602: \nL603:  | $$(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$$  |\nL604: by cite86†Theorem 5.1 , so in particular, cite129†1 holds. It follows from cite96†Theorem 4.6 cite130†2 that\nL605: \nL606:  | $${\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))}.$$  |\nL607: \nL608: Finally, because $\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$, cite131†2 must hold by cite120†Definition 4.4 .\nL609: Suppose now that cite129†1 and cite131†2 both hold. Then $\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))_{d}=\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})$ by cite120†Definition 4.4 , and therefore $\\mathsf{T}(\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})))=\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}$. cite96†Theorem 4.6 cite130†2 then implies that\nL610:  | $$\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})).$$  |\nL611: \nL612: We conclude that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$ by cite86†Theorem 5.1 . ∎\nL613: \nL614: We now characterize the 1-pop-stack sortable elements of $\\tors\\Lambda$.\nL615: ###### Corollary 5.4.\nL616: \nL617: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$. The following are equivalent.\nL618: \nL619:   1. (1)\nL620: \nL621: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\hat{0}$.\nL622: \nL623:   2. (2)\nL624: \nL625: Every brick in $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$ is simple.\nL626: \nL627:   3. (3)\nL628: \nL629: The torsion class $\\mathcal{T}$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL630: \nL631:   4. (4)\nL632: \nL633: There exists a nonzero injective module $I$ such that $\\mathcal{T}=\\prescript{\\perp}{}{I}$.\nL634: \nL635: Dually, the following are equivalent.\nL636: \nL637:   1. (1)\nL638: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL639: \nL640:   2. (2)\nL641: \nL642: Every brick in $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$ is simple.\nL643: \nL644:   3. (3)\nL645: \nL646: The torsion-free class $\\mathcal{T}^{\\perp}$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL647: \nL648:   4. (4)\nL649: \nL650: There exists a nonzero projective module $P$ such that $\\mathcal{T}=\\Gen(P)$.\nL651: ###### Proof.\nL652: \nL653: We prove only the first result as the second is dual. The equivalences among cite132†2 , cite133†3 , and cite134†4 are contained in cite135†Proposition 3.10 .\nL654: \nL655: To see that cite136†1 implies cite132†2 , suppose $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\hat{0}$. Then every brick in $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))$ is simple. But $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))$ by cite137†Theorem 5.3 .\nL656: We now show that cite133†3 implies cite136†1 . Suppose $\\mathcal{T}$ is a Serre subcategory. Then in particular, $\\mathcal{T}$ is closed under submodules. Thus, for $Y\\in\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$, the map $g_{Y,\\mathcal{X}}$ must be surjective. cite86†Theorem 5.1 then implies that $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))=\\emptyset$, so $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=0$. ∎\nL657: The following characterizes all 2-pop-stack sortable elements of $\\tors\\Lambda$; it also describes the image under $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ of each 2-pop-stack sortable element.\nL658: ###### Corollary 5.5.\nL659: \nL660: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$, and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be a set of simple modules. Then $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ if and only if the following all hold.\nL661: \nL662:   1. (1)\nL663: \nL664: We have $\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))=0=\\mathrm{Ext}^{1}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$.\nL665: \nL666:   2. (2)\nL667: The socle^{2}^{2} 2 Recall that the socle $\\mathrm{soc}M$ of a module $M$ is the sum of all of its semisimple submodules. $\\mathrm{soc}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ of $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$ satisfies $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})/\\mathrm{soc}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$.\nL668: \nL669:   3. (3)\nL670: \nL671: There does not exist $S\\in\\mathcal{S}$ with $\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),S)=0$. (Equivalently, there is a surjection $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\twoheadrightarrow\\mathcal{S}$.)\nL672: ###### Proof.\nL673: Suppose first that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$. Since $\\mathcal{D}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))=\\mathcal{S}$, it follows from cite137†Theorem 5.3 that $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$. Then the fact that cite138†1 holds follows from the definition of a 2-term simple-minded collection.\nL674: cite137†Theorem 5.3 also implies that for every $S\\in\\mathcal{S}$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ that admits a surjection $Z\\twoheadrightarrow S$. This shows that cite139†3 holds. To prove cite140†2 , consider ${X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))}$. Since $\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ is closed under submodules, the map $g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\colon X\\rightarrow S_{X}$ must be surjective.\nL675: Moreover, we have that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))=0$ by cite87†Corollary 5.4 . cite86†Theorem 5.1 implies that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ is in $\\mathcal{U}(0)$, which is precisely the set of simple modules. If follows that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\subseteq\\mathrm{soc}(X)$ and therefore that $X/\\mathrm{soc}(X)\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$.\nL676: To prove the converse, suppose $\\mathcal{T}$ satisfies cite138†Items 1 , cite140†2 and cite139†3 . Then cite138†1 implies that $(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ is a semibrick pair. Moreover, for $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, the map $g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ must be surjective since $\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ is closed under submodules. This map must also have a semisimple kernel by cite140†2 .\nL677: We then have that $\\mu_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}\\cup\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime})$ for $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}=\\{\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\mid X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\}$. cite141†Proposition A.3 therefore implies that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ is simple for all $X$.\nL678: Letting $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime}$ denote the set of all simple modules, we find that $(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime})$ is a 2-term simple-minded collection satisfying $\\mathcal{S}\\cup\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}\\subseteq\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime}$. By cite142†Proposition A.4 , $(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{Y}):=\\mu_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime})$ is a 2-term simple-minded collection satisfying $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{Y}$.\nL679: Now note that $\\mathcal{Y}=\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$ by cite112†Theorem 3.7 . Hence, $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$. Together with cite139†3 , this allows us to use cite137†Theorem 5.3 to conclude that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$. ∎\nL680: ### 5.3. The image of pop-stack\nL681: \nL682: We now build toward a complete description of the images of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ and $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ (and, hence, the facets of the canonical join complex of $\\tors\\Lambda$) for any $\\tau$-tilting finite algebra $\\Lambda$. Our first result is proven more generally for arbitrary semidistrim lattices in [cite59†DW23 , Section 9]. We give a new representation-theoretic proof for lattices of torsion classes.\nL683: ###### Proposition 5.6.\nL684: \nL685: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$.\nL686: \nL687:   1. (1)\nL688: \nL689: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\subseteq\\mathcal{T}$.\nL690: \nL691:   2. (2)\nL692: \nL693: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\supseteq\\mathcal{T}$.\nL694: \nL695:   3. (3)\nL696: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL697: \nL698:   4. (4)\nL699: \nL700: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL701: ###### Proof.\nL702: \nL703: We prove only cite143†1 and cite144†3 as the proofs of cite145†2 and cite146†4 are similar.\nL704: \nL705: We have $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))$ by cite86†Theorem 5.1 . By cite110†Theorem 3.4 , this implies cite143†1 .\nL706: To prove cite144†3 , let $(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))$. Let $(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})=\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})$ and $(\\mathcal{X}_{2},\\mathcal{Y}_{2})=\\mu_{\\uparrow}(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})$. By cite86†Theorem 5.1 , we need to show that $\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X}_{2},\\mathcal{Y}_{2})=(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})$.\nL707: By cite120†Definition 4.4 , this is equivalent to showing that $\\mathcal{X}_{2}=\\mathcal{Y}_{1}$, which is equivalent to showing that $g_{\\mathcal{Y}_{1},Z}$ is surjective for every $Z\\in\\mathcal{X}_{1}$.\nL708: Now recall that $\\mathcal{X}_{1}=\\{\\mathrm{coker}(g_{Y,\\mathcal{X}})\\mid Y\\in\\mathcal{Y}\\text{ and }g_{Y,\\mathcal{X}}\\text{ is injective}\\}$. Thus, for $Z\\in\\mathcal{X}_{1}$, we have a quotient map $X_{Y}\\twoheadrightarrow Z$ for some $X_{Y}\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{X})$. Since $\\mathcal{X}\\subseteq\\mathcal{Y}_{1}$, this map must factor through the minimal right $\\Filt(\\mathcal{Y}_{1})$-approximation $g_{\\mathcal{Y}_{1},Z}\\colon(Y_{1})_{Z}\\rightarrow Z$.\nL709: This implies that $g_{\\mathcal{Y}_{1},Z}$ must be surjective. ∎\nL710: As a consequence, we can characterize the image of the pop-stack operators as follows.\nL711: ###### Corollary 5.7.\nL712: \nL713: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$. Then the following are equivalent.\nL714: \nL715:   1. (1)\nL716: \nL717: There exists $\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$ such that $\\mathcal{T}=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})$.\nL718: \nL719:   2. (2)\nL720: \nL721: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))=\\mathcal{T}$.\nL722: \nL723:   3. (3)\nL724: \nL725: The semibricks $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$ and $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))$ coincide.\nL726:   4. (4)\nL727: \nL728: For all $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, the map $g_{\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}),X}$ is surjective.\nL729: \nL730:   5. (5)\nL731: \nL732: For all $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))$ such that there is a surjective map $Z\\twoheadrightarrow X$.\nL733: \nL734: Dually, the following are equivalent.\nL735: \nL804: ###### Theorem 5.10.\nL805: \nL806: Suppose $\\Lambda$ is hereditary, and let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$. Then $\\mathcal{T}$ is in the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ if and only if both of the following hold:\nL807: \nL808:   1. (1)\nL809: \nL810: $\\mathrm{Ext}^{1}_{\\Lambda}(X,X^{\\prime})=0$ for all $X,X^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL811: \nL812:   2. (2)\nL813: \nL814: There does not exist a nonzero projective module $P\\in\\mathcal{T}$.\nL827: Suppose that cite155†1 holds and that $\\mathcal{T}$ is not in the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$. By cite149†Corollary 5.7 , this means there exists $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$ such that $g_{\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}),X}$ is injective. We will prove that $X$ is projective by induction on $|\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})|$. First suppose $|\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})|=1$. Then cite86†Theorem 5.1 implies that ${|\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))|=|\\Lambda|}$.\nL828: Now, $|\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))|+|\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))|=|\\Lambda|$ by cite112†Theorem 3.7 and [cite81†KY14 , Corollary 5.5]^{3}^{3} 3 This is a classical and well-known fact that was proved much earlier than [cite81†KY14 ], but this logical deduction suits the organization of this paper., so $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL854: We conclude this section by combining several known results into a lattice-theoretic interpretation of the Ext-orthogonality condition in cite91†Theorem 5.10 . As we discuss in cite158†Remark 7.7 , a special case of this result can also be interpreted (and proved) using Coxeter combinatorics.\nL980: Associated to the acyclic orientation $Q_{c}$ of $c$ is a finite-dimensional algebra $KQ_{c}$ known as the tensor algebra of $Q_{c}$ (More precisely, the definition of $KQ_{c}$ depends on a choice of symmetrizable Cartan matrix with Weyl group $W$.) In this subsection, we recall the properties of $KQ_{c}$ that we will use to interpret cite91†Theorem 5.10 in terms of Coxeter combinatorics.\nL1020: In this section, we recast cite91†Theorem 5.10 as a combinatorial description of the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}$ for any finite irreducible Coxeter group $W$ and any Coxeter element $c$. As we discuss in the proof of cite92†Theorem 7.8 , the results of cite19†Section 5 only yield a proof for $W$ of crystallographic type, while the non-crystallographic types can be verified by computer.\nL1022: We first give an interpretation of the Ext-orthogonality condition in cite91†Theorem 5.10 cite155†1 . For each $w\\in\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$, cite171†Lemmas 5.12 and cite172†6.7 say that the torsion class $\\varphi_{c}(w)$ satisfies the Ext-orthogonality condition if and only if $[\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}(w),w]$ is Boolean. cite93†Theorem 7.6 below provides several equivalent conditions characterizing when this holds. Before we state and prove this result, we require some lemmas.\nL1114: We now turn toward interpreting cite153†Item 2 in cite91†Theorem 5.10 . Recall that $S$ is precisely the set of atoms of $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$. In the crystallographic case, cite172†Theorem 6.7 says that these atoms can be indexed (by $[n]$) so that $\\varphi_{c}(s_{i})=\\Filt(S_{i})$ for all $i$. In the non-crystallographic case, we fix an arbitrary indexing of $S$ by $[n]$. In either case, for $s_{i}\\in S$, we let\nL1140: In the language of representation theory, this means that there exist bricks $X,X^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\varphi(25314))$ such that ${\\mathrm{Ext}^{1}_{\\Pi(A_{4})}(X,X^{\\prime})\\neq 0}$ (the notation $\\varphi$ is from cite158†Remark 7.7 ). Thus, the naive analogue of cite91†Theorem 5.10 likewise fails for the preprojective algebra $\\Pi(A_{4})$.\nL1142: For example, if $W=A_{9}$ and $c=s_{1}s_{3}s_{5}s_{7}s_{9}s_{2}s_{4}s_{6}s_{8}$, then one can use [cite201†ABB+19 , Theorem 1] and cite202†Corollary 8.5 below to show that the permutation $1,2,8,10,6,9,4,5,3,7$ is in the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}$ but not the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}$.\n"
